In the context of the present discussions, it would seem that the point of =
semantic categorization is heuristic, not philosophical.  So, all that is n=
eeded is not complete accuracy but useful groupings (typically, similar pla=
ce structures, however that may be defined).  And useful for sentence const=
ruction and analysis (knowing how many places is always handy -- and what t=
hey likely mean) as well as general semantic area.  With a little vocabular=
y adjustment, I suppose a few dozen categories would be enough, though you =
need to show that they actually improve learning (which will be hard, since=
 we don't know much about learning Lojban now).


On Monday, September 1, 2014 9:56 AM, Gleki Arxokuna <gleki.is.my.name@gmai=
l.com> wrote:
=20


Semantical categorization of the universe can't be done since most concepts=
 have more than one useful tag.
Look at this attempt:
http://mw.lojban.org/index.php?title=3Dsemantic_categorization_of_gismu

It is not perfect and no attempt can be perfect.

Also read this:
http://www.alamut.com/subj/artiface/language/johnWilkins.html




2014-09-01 18:36 GMT+04:00 TR NS <transfire@gmail.com>:

I was thinking about stevo's `CV` categorized gismu (still would like to se=
e that, btw) and recently reading about the notion of taxonomic languages (=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philosophical_language) when a neat idea stru=
ck me. If the the first three letters of a gismu related all such words --f=
or the sake of argument let's say all fruit words started with `fru`, so th=
en `frupa` might mean apple, `furba` banana, `furca` strawberry, etc. then =
my idea is that there could be a special cmavo that created a "group contex=
t", call it `xi'o... xi'u` and then one could say thins like:
>
>
>    fruxi'o pa .e ba .e ca [xi'u]
>
>
>Which would be the same as
>
>
>    frupa .e fruba .e fruca
>
>
>
>Of course this cmavo is a little odd b/c it works more like an inflection.=
 And unfortunately Lojban doesn't have any sort of taxonomic vocabulary wha=
tsoever. So it doesn't really fly. But I thought it was a neat idea anyway,=
 so I thought I'd share.
>
>
>.trans.
